course: dictionary
lesson: 5191
title: The jackpot: the biggest prize
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have a plain meaning and a casual meaning. Context helps you tell which one is used. «The lottery is a game where people buy a ticket and pick numbers; the chance of the jackpot is about one in 45 million.» Here the jackpot is the big prize you could win, and the sentence says it is very hard to win.
explain: Look at the numbers, too. One in 45 million is a very small chance, so almost everyone wins nothing. Put your meaning into the sentence to check it: "the chance of the biggest prize is about one in 45 million" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Now the casual meaning. People say, «We hit the jackpot» when they have great luck or find exactly what they wanted, even if no money was won. The words around «hit the jackpot» tell you whether money is involved or whether it is just a happy moment.
fact: A jackpot is the biggest prize in a game, lottery or competition.
fact: A jackpot is often a large amount of money.
fact: «Hit the jackpot» is an informal phrase. It means to have great luck or success, or to find exactly what you wanted.
fact: In the UK's main Lotto, the chance of the jackpot is about 1 in 45 million for each ticket.
exercise: «The lottery is a game where people buy a ticket and pick numbers.» In this sentence, what is the jackpot? | The office where the numbers are picked | A number on the ticket | The price of one ticket | *The biggest prize in the game
exercise: «We hit the jackpot when we found the perfect house.» What does «hit the jackpot» mean here? | We got lost on the way home | *We had great luck and found exactly what we wanted | We won a game of lottery money | We broke a window by accident
exercise: «The chance of the jackpot is about one in 45 million.» What does this tell us? | Winning the jackpot is very easy | *Winning the jackpot is very hard | Everybody who buys a ticket wins | The jackpot is a free gift
test: «Each ticket has a chance of the jackpot, about one in 45 million.» Which word tells you the chance is very small? | Each | Ticket | Chance of the jackpot | *One in 45 million
test: Ben says, «We hit the jackpot!» after finding a £5 note in his coat. Is he using the phrase in its casual sense? | No, he means he lost his coat | Yes, he means he is a rich man now | No, he means he won the lottery jackpot | *Yes, he means he had great luck
exam: Mia says «we hit the jackpot» always means she won lots of money. Is she right? | Yes, but only in the UK | Yes, it always means money won in a game | No, it means losing everything | *No, it can mean great luck or finding exactly what you wanted
